<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ion of Chios</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 490–c. 420 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ion of Chios was a Greek poet and prose writer of the fifth century BCE who spent much of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. He wrote tragedies, lyric poetry and a work of reminiscences describing meetings with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a>, one of the earliest examples of memoir in Greek. He also recorded the island's foundation legends in a history of Chios.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ion was the son of Orthomenes and was surnamed the son of Xuthus, probably a nickname alluding to Xuthus, the father of the mythical Ion. Very young, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where he enjoyed the society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> and left laudatory notices of him in works that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> quotes. Plutarch also reports that Ion severely criticised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>, said to have been his rival in love. Ion himself recorded that he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a> on Chios in 440 BCE, when Sophocles was commander of the expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>.</p><p>His first tragedy was produced in 452 BCE. About 429 BCE he took third place in competition with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> and Iophon, and he died before 421 BCE, as the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>, produced that year, shows. Only one victory is recorded. Having won the dithyrambic and tragic prizes at the same time, he is said to have given every Athenian a pitcher of Chian wine, which suggests that he was wealthy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The number of his tragedies is given as 12, 30 or 40. The titles and a few fragments of eleven plays survive, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>, Alcmene, Laertes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teucer">Teucer</a> and the satyric drama Omphale. Pseudo-Longinus described his style as marked by petty refinements and a want of boldness. Even so, he was greatly admired, apparently for an elegant wit. Arcesilaus, Baton of Sinope, Didymus, Epigenes and Aristarchus wrote commentaries on him. According to a scholiast on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> he also wrote lyric poems, comedies, epigrams, paeans, hymns and elegies, and some remnants of the elegies are in the Greek Anthology.</p><p>His prose included the Chiou Ktisis, a historical work in the Ionic dialect, apparently in imitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>. He also wrote the Triagmos, a philosophical treatise on the constitution of things according to a theory of triads, whose few fragments suggest Pythagorean leanings. A work called Hypomnemata is sometimes identified with the Epidemiai, which contained either an account of his own travels or of the visits of well-known people to Chios.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ion of Chios” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek tradition, reported by the fifth-century Chian writer <b>Ion of Chios</b>, made Oenopion, a son of Dionysus who came from Crete, the island's first king and the man who taught its people to make wine, and spoke of later Carian and Abantian settlers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chios, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.4,26.02&amp;year=-445" aria-label="Chios on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5197 785 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ion of Chios"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.4,26.02&amp;year=-445">What was here in 445 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
